#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:9
msgid "From Python 3.3 to today: ending 15 years of subprocess polling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:11
msgid ""
"One of the less fun aspects of process management on POSIX systems is "
"waiting for a process to terminate. The standard library's :mod:`subprocess` "
"module has relied on a busy-loop polling approach since the *timeout* "
"parameter was added to :meth:`subprocess.Popen.wait` in Python 3.3, around "
"15 years ago (see `source <https://github.com/python/cpython/"
"blob/8def603d853c7f5e4ff57f95de289f99e1943669/Lib/subprocess.py#L2056-"
"L2077>`__). And psutil's :meth:`Process.wait` method uses exactly the same "
"technique (see `source <https://github.com/giampaolo/psutil/blob/700b7e6a/"
"psutil/_psposix.py#L95-L160>`__)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:19
msgid ""
"The logic is straightforward: check whether the process has exited using non-"
"blocking ``waitpid(WNOHANG)``, sleep briefly, check again, sleep a bit "
"longer, and so on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:23
msgid ""
"import os, time\n"
"\n"
"def wait_busy(pid, timeout):\n"
"    end = time.monotonic() + timeout\n"
"    interval = 0.0001\n"
"    while time.monotonic() < end:\n"
"        pid_done, _ = os.waitpid(pid, os.WNOHANG)\n"
"        if pid_done:\n"
"            return\n"
"        time.sleep(interval)\n"
"        interval = min(interval * 2, 0.04)\n"
"    raise TimeoutExpired"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:38
msgid ""
"In this blog post I'll show how I finally addressed this long-standing "
"inefficiency, first in psutil, and most excitingly, directly in CPython's "
"standard library :mod:`subprocess` module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:43
msgid "The problem with busy-polling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:45
msgid ""
"CPU wake-ups: even with exponential backoff (starting at 0.1ms, capping at "
"40ms), the system constantly wakes up to check process status, wasting CPU "
"cycles and draining batteries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:48
msgid ""
"Latency: there's always a gap between when a process actually terminates and "
"when you detect it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:50
msgid ""
"Scalability: monitoring many processes simultaneously magnifies all of the "
"above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:54
msgid "Event-driven waiting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:56
msgid ""
"All POSIX systems provide at least one mechanism to be notified when a file "
"descriptor becomes ready. These are :manpage:`select(2)`, :manpage:"
"`poll(2)`, :manpage:`epoll(7)` (Linux) and `kqueue() <https://man.freebsd."
"org/cgi/man.cgi?query=kqueue>`__ (BSD / macOS) system calls. Until recently, "
"I believed they could only be used with file descriptors referencing "
"sockets, pipes, etc., but it turns out they can also be used to wait for "
"events on process PIDs!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:65
msgid "Linux"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:67
msgid ""
"In 2019, Linux 5.3 introduced a new syscall, :func:`os.pidfd_open`, which "
"was added in Python 3.9. It returns a :term:`file descriptor` referencing a "
"process PID. The interesting thing is that :manpage:`pidfd_open(2)` can be "
"used in conjunction with :manpage:`select(2)`, :manpage:`poll(2)` or :"
"manpage:`epoll(7)` to effectively wait until the process exits. E.g. by "
"using ``poll()``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:74
msgid ""
"import os, select\n"
"\n"
"def wait_pidfd(pid, timeout):\n"
"    pidfd = os.pidfd_open(pid)\n"
"    poller = select.poll()\n"
"    poller.register(pidfd, select.POLLIN)\n"
"    # block until process exits or timeout occurs\n"
"    events = poller.poll(timeout * 1000)\n"
"    if events:\n"
"        return\n"
"    raise TimeoutError"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:88
msgid ""
"This approach has zero busy-looping. The kernel wakes us up exactly when the "
"process terminates or when the timeout expires if the PID is still alive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:91
msgid ""
"I chose ``poll()`` over ``select()`` because ``select()`` has a historical "
"file descriptor limit (``FD_SETSIZE``), which typically caps it at 1024 :"
"term:`file descriptors <file descriptor>` per-process (reminded me of :bpo:"
"`1685000`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:96
msgid ""
"I chose ``poll()`` over ``epoll()`` because it does not require creating an "
"additional :term:`file descriptor`. It also needs only a single syscall, "
"which should make it a bit more efficient when monitoring a single FD rather "
"than many."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:102
msgid "macOS and BSD"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:104
msgid ""
"BSD-derived systems (including macOS) provide the ``kqueue()`` syscall. It's "
"conceptually similar to ``select()``, ``poll()`` and ``epoll()``, but more "
"powerful (e.g. it can also handle regular files). ``kqueue()`` can be passed "
"a PID directly, and it will return once the PID disappears or the timeout "
"expires:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:110
msgid ""
"import select\n"
"\n"
"def wait_kqueue(pid, timeout):\n"
"    kq = select.kqueue()\n"
"    kev = select.kevent(\n"
"        pid,\n"
"        filter=select.KQ_FILTER_PROC,\n"
"        flags=select.KQ_EV_ADD | select.KQ_EV_ONESHOT,\n"
"        fflags=select.KQ_NOTE_EXIT,\n"
"    )\n"
"    # block until process exits or timeout occurs\n"
"    events = kq.control([kev], 1, timeout)\n"
"    if events:\n"
"        return\n"
"    raise TimeoutError"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:129
msgid "Windows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:131
msgid ""
"Windows does not busy-loop, both in psutil and :mod:`subprocess` module, "
"thanks to ``WaitForSingleObject``. This means Windows has effectively had "
"event-driven process waiting from the start. So nothing to do on that front."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:136
msgid "Graceful fallbacks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:138
msgid ""
"Both ``pidfd_open()`` and ``kqueue()`` can fail for different reasons. For "
"example, with ``EMFILE`` if the process runs out of :term:`file descriptors "
"<file descriptor>` (usually 1024), or with ``EACCES`` / ``EPERM`` if the "
"syscall was explicitly blocked at the system level by the sysadmin (e.g. via "
"SECCOMP). In all cases, psutil silently falls back to the traditional busy-"
"loop polling approach rather than raising an exception."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:145
msgid ""
"This fast-path-with-fallback approach is similar in spirit to :bpo:`33671`, "
"where I sped up :func:`shutil.copyfile` by using zero-copy system calls back "
"in 2018. In there, more efficient :func:`os.sendfile` is attempted first, "
"and if it fails (e.g. on network filesystems) we fall back to the "
"traditional :manpage:`read(2)` / :manpage:`write(2)` approach to copy "
"regular files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:152
msgid "Measurement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:154
msgid ""
"As a simple experiment, here's a simple program which waits on itself for 10 "
"seconds without terminating:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:157
msgid ""
"# test.py\n"
"import psutil, os\n"
"try:\n"
"    psutil.Process(os.getpid()).wait(timeout=10)\n"
"except psutil.TimeoutExpired:\n"
"    pass"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:166
msgid ""
"We can measure the CPU context switching using ``/usr/bin/time -v``. Before "
"the patch (the busy-loop):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:171
msgid ""
"$ /usr/bin/time -v python3 test.py 2>&1 | grep context\n"
"    Voluntary context switches: 258\n"
"    Involuntary context switches: 4"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:175
msgid "After the patch (the event-driven approach):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:179
msgid ""
"$ /usr/bin/time -v python3 test.py 2>&1 | grep context\n"
"    Voluntary context switches: 2\n"
"    Involuntary context switches: 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:183
msgid ""
"This shows that instead of spinning in userspace, the process blocks in "
"``poll()`` / ``kqueue()``, and is woken up only when the kernel notifies it, "
"resulting in just a few CPU :term:`context switches <context switch>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:188
msgid "Sleeping state"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:190
msgid ""
"It's also interesting to note that waiting via ``poll()`` (or ``kqueue()``) "
"puts the process into the exact same sleeping state as a plain :func:`time."
"sleep` call. From the kernel's perspective, both are interruptible sleeps: "
"the process is de-scheduled, consumes zero CPU, and sits quietly in kernel "
"space."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:196
msgid ""
"The ``\"S+\"`` state shown below by ``ps`` means that the process “sleeps in "
"foreground”."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:199
msgid ":func:`time.sleep`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:203
msgid ""
"$ (python3 -c 'import time; time.sleep(10)' & pid=$!; sleep 0.3; ps -o pid,"
"stat,comm -p $pid) && fg &>/dev/null\n"
"    PID STAT COMMAND\n"
" 491573 S+   python3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:207
msgid ":func:`select.poll`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:211
msgid ""
"$ (python3 -c 'import os,select; fd = os.pidfd_open(os.getpid(),0); p = "
"select.poll(); p.register(fd,select.POLLIN); p.poll(10_000)' & pid=$!; sleep "
"0.3; ps -o pid,stat,comm -p $pid) && fg &>/dev/null\n"
"    PID STAT COMMAND\n"
" 491748 S+   python3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:216
msgid "CPython contribution"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:218
msgid ""
"After landing the psutil implementation (:pr:`2706`), I took the extra step "
"and submitted a matching pull request for CPython :mod:`subprocess` module: :"
"cpy-pr:`144047`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:222
msgid ""
"I'm especially proud of this one: this is the **third time** in psutil's 17+ "
"year history that a feature developed in psutil made its way upstream into "
"the Python standard library."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:226
msgid ""
"The first was back in 2010, when :meth:`Process.nice` inspired :func:`os."
"getpriority` and :func:`os.setpriority`, see :bpo:`10784`. Landed in Python "
"3.3."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:230
msgid ""
"The second was back in 2011, when :func:`psutil.disk_usage` inspired :func:"
"`shutil.disk_usage`, see `python-ideas ML proposal <https://mail.python.org/"
"archives/list/python-ideas@python.org/"
"thread/67A7ML2TJ7MBS3WOL6IZKLD2C3B3VCQG>`__. Landed in Python 3.3."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2026/event-driven-process-waiting.rst:235
msgid ""
"*Funny thing:* 15 years ago, Python 3.3 added the *timeout* parameter to :"
"meth:`subprocess.Popen.wait` (see `commit <https://github.com/python/cpython/"
"commit/31aa7dd1419>`__). That's probably where I took inspiration when I "
"first added the *timeout* parameter to psutil's :meth:`Process.wait` around "
"the same time (see :commit:`886710daf`). Now, 15 years later, I'm "
"contributing back a similar improvement for that very same *timeout* "
"parameter. **The circle is complete**."
msgstr ""

